<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yakuts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sakha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yakuts, who call themselves Sakha, are a Turkic people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> living mainly along the Lena River. They traditionally raised horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> in a harsh subarctic climate and came under Russian rule in the seventeenth century. In 1922 the Yakut Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic was created; as the Sakha Republic it is today the largest subdivision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yakuts.jpg" alt="Yakuts" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lyudmila Sleptsova, Mikhail Starostin, Pyotr Zakharov (Людмила Слепцова, Михаил Старостин, Пётр Захаров) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sakha.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars have proposed several origins for the Yakuts. In the 19th century Nikolai Schukin, working from local oral histories, introduced the view that Khakas groups from the southern Yenisey migrated north to the Lena, and Nikolai Kozmin gave the most authoritative support to this Yenisey hypothesis in 1928.</p><p>Many researchers conclude that the Yakuts arose from Turkic migrants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> region mixing with the native Yukaghir and Tungusic peoples of the Lena. Yakut tradition remembers earlier peoples such as the Tumats, with whom there was conflict and some intermarriage. According to one account the Yakuts began to move to the basins of the Middle Lena, the Aldan and the Vilyuy from the 13th century, under pressure from the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1620s the Tsardom of Muscovy moved into Yakut territory, imposed a fur tax called yasak and put down several Yakut rebellions between 1634 and 1642. Brutal collection of the tax provoked a rebellion in 1642 among the Yakuts and the Tungusic-speaking tribes along the Lena. The voivode Peter Golovin answered with a reign of terror in which settlements were burned and hundreds killed. The Yakut population is estimated to have fallen by 70 percent between 1642 and 1682, mainly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other infectious diseases.</p><p>Gold and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> brought more Russians. By the 1820s almost all Yakuts claimed to have converted to the Russian Orthodox church, though they kept a number of tengri practices. Yakut literature began to rise in the late 19th century, and a national revival followed in the early 20th. After the Yakut Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic was created in 1922, collectivisation brought systematic persecution from the late 1920s to the late 1930s, and the Yakut population fell from 240,500 in 1926 to 236,700 in 1959 before recovering.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Yakuts practise animal husbandry. They have traditionally raised horses, reindeer and a hardy breed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> suited to the harsh climate. According to one account, northern Yakuts were largely hunters, fishermen and reindeer herders, while the southern Yakuts raised cattle and horses. Their language, Yakut or Sakha, belongs to the northern branch of the Siberian Turkic languages and is most closely related to Dolgan. Their cuisine includes the fermented mare's-milk drink kumis, dairy products, stroganina, which is sliced frozen salted fish, horse meat and salamat, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> porridge with butter and horse fat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yakuts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its creation of a federation of national units, with autonomous republics for peoples such as the Tatars, Bashkirs and <b>Yakuts</b>, set the pattern of Soviet nationality policy and survives in the republics of today’s Russian Federation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
